#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# extract

#% text
# `extract(x, pos)` gives the part of `x` at the position `pos`, a list of indices `[i, j, ...]` as `position` gives them.

#% text
# `extract(x, [pos1, pos2, ...])` gives the list of the parts at several positions.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Indices count from 1, negative from the end; 0 is the head.

#% item
# `extract(x, [i, j])` is `x[i, j]`; with a list of positions it pairs naturally with `position`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
extract([[a, b], [c, d]], [2, 1])
#% output
#= c

#% cell
extract([[a, b], [c, d]], [[1, 2], [2, 2]])
#% output
#= [b, d]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The head and negative indices:

#% cell
extract(f(x, y), 0)
#% output
#= f

#% cell
extract([a, b, c], -1)
#% output
#= c

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The parts `position` finds:

#% cell
t = [1, x, [2, y]]
extract(t, position(t, _: Sym))
#% output
#= [list, x, list, y]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `part`, `position`, `replace_part`, `delete`
